  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040800/assets/32500120ed36acde90f5b27c44a6cf45f2d4766bacb5eb097debc0cd7117f27f.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?",
  "worked_solution": "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x,\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(27-\\frac{9}{2})-(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $22\\,\\text{kg}.$",
